@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.1795 = private unnamed_addr constant [1744 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for missing `from __future__ import annotations` imports upon\0Adetecting type annotations that can be written more succinctly under\0APEP 563.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APEP 585 enabled the use of a number of convenient type annotations, such as\0A`list[str]` instead of `List[str]`. However, these annotations are only\0Aavailable on Python 3.9 and higher, _unless_ the `from __future__ import annotations`\0Aimport is present.\0A\0ASimilarly, PEP 604 enabled the use of the `|` operator for unions, such as\0A`str | None` instead of `Optional[str]`. However, these annotations are only\0Aavailable on Python 3.10 and higher, _unless_ the `from __future__ import annotations`\0Aimport is present.\0A\0ABy adding the `__future__` import, the pyupgrade rules can automatically\0Amigrate existing code to use the new syntax, even for older Python versions.\0AThis rule thus pairs well with pyupgrade and with Ruff's pyupgrade rules.\0A\0AThis rule respects the [`target-version`] setting. For example, if your\0Aproject targets Python 3.10 and above, adding `from __future__ import annotations`\0Adoes not impact your ability to leverage PEP 604-style unions (e.g., to\0Aconvert `Optional[str]` to `str | None`). As such, this rule will only\0Aflag such usages if your project targets Python 3.9 or below.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import List, Dict, Optional\0A\0A\0Adef func(obj: Dict[str, Optional[int]]) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import annotations\0A\0Afrom typing import List, Dict, Optional\0A\0A\0Adef func(obj: Dict[str, Optional[int]]) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0AAfter running the additional pyupgrade rules:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import annotations\0A\0A\0Adef func(obj: dict[str, int | None]) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A", align 1
