@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2014 = private unnamed_addr constant [857 x i8] c"## What it does\0ACheck for an invalid `mode` argument in `open` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `open` function accepts a `mode` argument that specifies how the file\0Ashould be opened (e.g., read-only, write-only, append-only, etc.).\0A\0APython supports a variety of open modes: `r`, `w`, `a`, and `x`, to control\0Areading, writing, appending, and creating, respectively, along with\0A`b` (binary mode), `+` (read and write), and `U` (universal newlines),\0Athe latter of which is only valid alongside `r`. This rule detects both\0Ainvalid combinations of modes and invalid characters in the mode string\0Aitself.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Awith open(\22file\22, \22rwx\22) as f:\0A    return f.read()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Awith open(\22file\22, \22r\22) as f:\0A    return f.read()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `open`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#open)\0A", align 1
